  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday Night through Friday)\nIssued at 810 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nZonal flow aloft Monday night gives way to weak, brief ridging for \nmuch of Tuesday. However, models suggest that an upper trough will \ndig and amplify towards the Southeast through midweek before swinging\nnortheast for late week. The onset timing of this feature has come \ninto better agreement among synoptic guidance; but, there remains a \nlot of disagreement on its overall evolution. Therefore, this will be\nsomething to keep an eye on over the coming days. At the surface, \nhigh pressure over the Midwest and Minnesota/Wisconsin areas will try\nto push towards the Southeast early in the week. However, as a low \npressure system develops over the eastern Gulf and pivots across the \nFlorida Peninsula into the Atlantic Wednesday morning, an inverted \ntrough looks to form over Alabama and up into the Ohio Valley \n(coincident with the aforementioned upper trough). As this low \npressure system off the east coast of FL translates to the northeast \nthrough late week, high pressure looks to build back over the South \nThursday into Friday.\n\nUltimately, expect the main window of shower activity to be late \nTuesday night through Wednesday evening (low to medium, 30-60%). But,\nwith high pressure building in behind this, dry weather is forecast \nfor Thursday through Friday. As for any storm potential, confidence \ncontinues to be low, as models are pessimistic about the amount of \nshear and instability needed for storm development. We'll continue to\nkeep an eye on this, but check back for updates, especially with any\nupcoming Thanksgiving holiday travel! As for temperatures, a \ncooldown is in store by late week, with highs topping out in the 50s \nThursday/Friday with lows in the 30s (and potentially even the upper \n20s in some spots) Thursday night.",
